== SIBLING PARITY ==
  The IRQ Clear write (edited case, +0x18c) vs the IRQ Set write (+0x188, NOT edited, shares no case with a decline):
    the same drive test `if (d->irq_status & d->irq_enable)`.  X14's operand and single-line cells are rowed at the
    Clear write only.  At the Set write X9 reaches only (and != 0) and (both zero), so `&&` there passes.  OWED
    SENTENCE, written into NOT COVERED (outside the floor, like fbenablewidth).
  The FIQ Clear write: no drive test (no FIQ output is wired); nothing owed.
  X14's DEVINIT/tick rows vs X9's legal rows: the same five reads, one Set, one Clear, the five declines -- parity by
    construction; widths 1/2 and the other writes stay X9's (the instance axis is rowed at len 4 only -- contract).

== CLAIM -> ROW (added comment sentences asserting an observable) ==
  X14 header "Every row here that checks a Clear write's drive also requires the shared pair UNMOVED" -> ZCPU() on
    the 11 rows that check a Clear's drive (2 operand-cell, 7 single-line, the 2 line-4 Clears) and on the line-4
    IRQ_STATUS row (the producer's own assert, counted on the CPU pair).  The DEVINIT/tick Set+Clear rows and the
    re-arm row do not check the drive (enable value; announce count) -- the header says "checks a Clear write's
    drive" for that reason.
  X14 header "irq_asserted was never 1: its only writer is footbridge_interrupt_assert(), which no block called" ->
    grep -c 'footbridge_interrupt_assert' regress/diff_footbridge.c (707 rows) = 0; the r3x draft = 4 (its helper
    and comments).  Run at 18:31:10.  irq_asserted's writers in dev_footbridge.c: :395 (= 1) and :410 (= 0) only.
  contract "the lines a device raises are the timers (4-7), ISA 10 (cats) and 11 (netwinder) and PCI 18 (cats); no
    device raises the other 25" -> DEVINIT's INTERRUPT_CONNECT for 4-7; bus_pci.c ".10.isa" (cats), irq_path_isa
    ".11" (netwinder), `irq = 18` (cats dec21143).  The 25 are disclosed, not rowed.
  contract "an 8-byte access to +0x184, +0x18c or +0x284 cannot happen" -> the Opus seat's N_L08/N_L11 (equivalent
    for guests after its alignment correction); no row -- it is a domain statement.
  NOT COVERED "the IRQ Set write's own drive test ... `&&` there passes" -> a prediction, not a claim of coverage.

== GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD ==
  The edited guards read writeflag and their own latch; their bodies read nothing but that latch (declines) or the
  four interrupt fields (legal branches).  The fields the Opus seat conditioned on (irq_asserted, the timer fields,
  pending ticks) are read by NO branch: the disagreeing states owed are exactly X14's instances -- DEVINIT values,
  a tick pending, irq_asserted = 1 through the producer.  Writers checked: irq_asserted only in
  footbridge_interrupt_assert/deassert (grep); timer_control/timer_load in DEVINIT and the timer arms; pending in the
  timer callback and the timer arms.

== CHANNEL: each X14 row, the variant it is for, the observable that differs ==
  "X14 Clear 0x10 from 0x18/0x30 (disjoint)"   N_S01, N_S02, N_I06: `&&` asserts (cpu_asserts 1, want 0); a timer
                                                deassert moves the shared pair (d0+1) and leaves cpu_deasserts 0.
  "X14 Clear 0x10 from 0/0x30 (no status)"     N_S02 (enable alone asserts), and any (status || enable) form.
  "X14 Clear 0, only line L pending ..." x7    N_SB05/06/07/10/11/18, N_S06/N_S07: masked line -> deassert instead
                                                of assert; N_I05: a timer assert moves the shared pair, cpu 0.
  "X14 DEVINIT timers: the five legal reads"   N_T04, N_T05: the diverted read declines -> reads 0 and announces.
  "X14 DEVINIT timers: Set 0x40 then Clear"    N_F09: the fetch skipped -> idata 0 -> enable 0x70 not 0x60.
  "X14 DEVINIT timers: the five declines"      N_T09 (announce count 4), F1 (carried: +0x184 decline silent -> 4).
  the three ", a tick pending" rows            N_T06 (the Clear declined as a read: enable 0x70, announce 1),
                                                N_F15 (idata 0 -> 0x70).
  "X14 line 4 ...: IRQ_STATUS reads 0x10"      N_T11 (reads 0, announces), V3 and e_dir_A0_irq_* (enable/status
                                                == 0x10 divert the read).
  "X14 line 4 ...: Clear 0x10 clears it"       N_T12, N_F01 (enable stays 0x10), N_I02 (no deassert), N_I04
                                                (asserts), e_dir_A2_irq_* (declined as a read).
  "X14 line 4 ...: Clear 0x400 keeps it"       N_I01 (deasserts instead of asserting).
  "X14 line 4 ...: 5 declines, then ..."       N_R05 (the second round announces 1), N_T10 and e_flag_A*_irq_*
                                                (the first round announces 4).
  Every X14 row's named channel VARIES under its named variant: each is a count or a field value the variant
  changes, never a survival oracle.

